const unit9={
id:9,title:'Physical activity and sports nutrition',headline:'Move with purpose.<br>Fuel every system.',intro:'Learn how training supports health, how muscles regenerate ATP, and how food and fluid help people perform and recover across 12 topics.',quizNote:'All 30 questions and answer choices match your submitted ALIVE Lesson Test 9 from October 26, 2026. The source answer key—including Questions 9, 19 and 27—is preserved; the explanations are hōlus. study notes added to support learning and current context.',
lessons:[
['Why physical activity matters','Connect movement with disease prevention and long-term function.','<p>Regular physical activity supports cardiovascular, metabolic, musculoskeletal and mental health. It lowers the risk of hypertension, stroke, cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, osteoporosis and several cancers. Benefits can begin below the full guideline target; more movement and less sitting both matter.</p><p>Current evidence links greater activity with lower risk of at least eight cancers, including breast, colon, endometrial and lung cancer. The source quiz uses an older, shorter list, so learn the principle rather than treating that list as exhaustive.</p><div class="example"><b>Movement as prevention</b><p>A brisk walk can improve cardiorespiratory fitness and energy expenditure. Resistance and weight-bearing work add muscle and bone benefits. A balanced program combines them.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.cdc.gov/physical-activity-basics/benefits/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CDC: benefits of physical activity</a></p>'],
['Exercise modes, bones and body composition','Match the training method to the adaptation.','<p><b>Aerobic activity</b> such as swimming, brisk walking or cycling challenges the heart, lungs and circulation. <b>Resistance training</b> provides overload that can increase strength and muscle mass. <b>Weight-bearing and impact activity</b> stimulate bone and help preserve function when appropriate for the person.</p><table><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Useful training</th></tr><tr><td>Cardiorespiratory fitness</td><td>Rhythmic aerobic activity using large muscle groups</td></tr><tr><td>Muscle mass and strength</td><td>Progressive resistance training</td></tr><tr><td>Bone health</td><td>Weight-bearing, impact and resistance activity</td></tr><tr><td>Mobility</td><td>Flexibility, balance and range-of-motion work</td></tr></table><p>Body-fat change depends on sustained energy balance, not one “best” exercise. Brisk walking can contribute to a deficit; resistance training helps preserve or build lean tissue.</p>'],
['Intensity and the energy cost of activity','Read exertion as one part of a complete prescription.','<p><b>Intensity</b> describes how hard the body works during an activity. Frequency, intensity, time and type form the FITT framework. Repetition and exhaustion are not substitutes for intensity.</p><p>Physical activity is the most variable component of daily energy expenditure. The source framework estimates roughly 15-30% for an “average” adult, so 2,700 kcal × 0.15-0.30 gives about <b>405-810 kcal</b>. A real person may be far outside that range.</p><div class="example"><b>Resting energy</b><p>A common textbook estimate is 1-1.5 kcal per minute at rest, or about 1,440-2,160 kcal per day. Body size, lean mass, age, physiology and measurement conditions change the value.</p></div><p>Use estimates as starting points, not as precise exercise-calorie credits.</p>'],
['Three ATP energy systems','Order them by rate, capacity and dominant duration.','<p>All movement requires ATP. The energy systems operate together, but their relative contributions shift with intensity and duration.</p><table><tr><th>System</th><th>Main feature</th><th>Typical role</th></tr><tr><td>ATP-phosphocreatine</td><td>Very fast, very limited capacity</td><td>Immediate explosive effort</td></tr><tr><td>Anaerobic glycolysis</td><td>Fast ATP from glucose or glycogen</td><td>Short, intense effort</td></tr><tr><td>Oxidative metabolism</td><td>Slower, high capacity; uses oxygen</td><td>Longer-duration work and recovery</td></tr></table><p>The quiz sequence is ATP-CP, glycolysis, then the citric acid cycle. “Anaerobic is simple” is shorthand for a lower-capacity set of pathways that produces ATP rapidly; it is not a claim that the biochemistry is literally simple.</p>'],
['Walking and oxidative metabolism','Follow fuels through mitochondria during steady activity.','<p>During steady walking, oxidative metabolism supplies most ATP. Acetyl-CoA enters the <b>citric acid cycle</b>, reducing equivalents feed the <b>electron transport chain</b>, and oxygen accepts electrons at the end. Fatty acids can enter through <b>beta-oxidation</b>.</p><p>Glycolysis still operates, especially as intensity changes, but the ATP-CP system is not the dominant sustained pathway. Fat and carbohydrate contributions shift with pace, training status, meal timing and duration.</p><div class="example"><b>Why multiple fuels matter</b><p>Muscles never rely solely on stored glycogen. Blood glucose, muscle glycogen, fatty acids and smaller contributions from other substrates can all support ATP production.</p></div>'],
['Sprinting, glycolysis and phosphocreatine','Explain why a 100-metre race needs fast systems.','<p>A maximal sprint demands ATP faster than oxidative metabolism alone can supply it. Stored ATP and the <b>ATP-CP system</b> dominate the opening seconds, while rapid glycolysis contributes increasingly as the effort continues.</p><p>Phosphocreatine donates phosphate to ADP through creatine kinase, rapidly regenerating ATP. Glycolysis breaks down glucose or glycogen and can produce ATP without oxygen being the immediate limiting factor.</p><div class="example"><b>100-metre race</b><p>The best source answer is ATP-CP plus glycolysis. Aerobic metabolism still contributes, but it is not the principal supplier in such a short maximal event.</p></div><p>Rest between repeated sprints helps restore phosphocreatine, which is one reason work-to-rest structure matters.</p>'],
['Creatine: synthesis, storage and supplements','Separate physiology from performance claims.','<p>Creatine is synthesized through an inter-organ pathway involving the <b>kidneys, pancreas and liver</b>. Most body creatine is stored in skeletal muscle as free creatine and phosphocreatine.</p><p>Creatine monohydrate can improve strength, power and repeated high-intensity performance for many people. Evidence is much weaker for steady endurance events such as marathon running, and added water mass may be undesirable for some endurance athletes.</p><div class="example"><b>Use the right performance question</b><p>A sprinter or lifter may benefit from a larger phosphocreatine pool. A marathon runner relies mainly on oxidative metabolism, so creatine is not a direct substitute for endurance training or adequate carbohydrate.</p></div><p>Supplement decisions should consider quality, dose, health history and sport rules with a qualified professional.</p><p><a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/ExerciseAndAthleticPerformance-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH ODS: exercise and performance supplements</a></p>'],
['Prolonged exercise and “hitting the wall”','Protect carbohydrate availability while using multiple fuels.','<p>During prolonged activity, liver <b>glycogenolysis</b> and <b>gluconeogenesis</b> help maintain blood glucose for active muscle and the nervous system. Muscle glycogen provides local fuel, while fat oxidation supplies an increasing share at lower and moderate intensities.</p><p>“Hitting the wall” describes profound fatigue associated with very low glycogen availability and falling carbohydrate oxidation. It is not simply a buildup of carbon dioxide or a lack of lactate.</p><div class="example"><b>Fuel before empty</b><p>Starting with adequate glycogen, pacing well and taking carbohydrate during long events can delay fatigue. Exact needs depend on duration, intensity, gut tolerance and the athlete.</p></div><p>Training improves the capacity to use and conserve fuels, but it does not make glycogen unlimited.</p>'],
['Training adaptations and interval work','Connect the workout stimulus with oxygen delivery and capacity.','<p>Endurance training increases mitochondrial capacity, capillarization and plasma volume and can expand total red-cell mass. These adaptations support oxygen delivery and use. Red blood cells carry oxygen through haemoglobin; capillaries improve exchange but do not themselves change the oxygen-carrying capacity of a given volume of blood.</p><p><b>Interval training</b> alternates harder work with recovery. It can challenge both aerobic and anaerobic systems, making it the best source answer for improving both capacities.</p><div class="example"><b>Intervals are adjustable</b><p>Short hard efforts emphasize power and glycolysis. Longer intervals near maximal sustainable aerobic intensity emphasize oxygen uptake. Recovery length changes the adaptation.</p></div><p>Progressive overload and recovery are essential; more intensity is not automatically better.</p>'],
['Athlete nutrition foundations','Build sport nutrition on an adequate everyday pattern.','<p>Athletes and nonathletes benefit from the same foundation: enough energy, varied minimally processed foods, carbohydrate-rich plants, quality protein, healthful fats and adequate micronutrients. Athletes often need different quantities, timing and convenience strategies.</p><p>Carbohydrate supports blood glucose and <b>glycogen</b> stores. The adult Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range is <b>45-65% of energy</b>, but sport-specific planning is often expressed in grams per kilogram because needs vary with training load.</p><div class="example"><b>How to read Question 21</b><p>The submitted course key is that athlete and nonathlete diets are similar at the pattern level. Many athletes also need substantially more protein than the 0.8 g/kg adult RDA, but a fixed “1.5 times” is not universal, so context matters.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10872/chapter/7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Academies: carbohydrate AMDR</a></p>'],
['Micronutrients and everyday hydration','Know the roles and the meaning of total water.','<p><b>Iron</b> is required for haemoglobin and oxygen delivery. <b>Calcium</b>, vitamin D, protein and mechanical loading support bone. <b>Magnesium</b> participates in ATP-related reactions, nerve and muscle function and protein synthesis.</p><p>The adult adequate intake for <b>total water</b> is about 2.7 L/day for women and 3.7 L/day for men in the source framework. Total water includes beverages and food moisture; it is not a universal plain-water prescription.</p><div class="example"><b>Needs move with conditions</b><p>Heat, altitude, pregnancy, lactation, illness, body size and exercise can change fluid needs. Thirst and urine colour can be useful practical clues, while pre/post exercise body mass helps estimate sweat loss.</p></div><p><a href="https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/10925/chapter/25" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Academies: water reference intakes</a></p>'],
['Hydration, sports drinks and heat illness','Replace losses without turning a rule of thumb into a rigid target.','<p>Dehydration reduces plasma volume, raises cardiovascular strain and can produce a rapid, weak pulse. Severe fluid loss can cause dry skin, confusion and loss of consciousness. Confusion or fainting in heat can also indicate <b>heat stroke</b>, a medical emergency requiring immediate cooling and emergency care.</p><p>Sports drinks can supply fluid, carbohydrate and electrolytes. They are often useful during endurance or intermittent exercise lasting about an hour or more, and sometimes during shorter intense work, heavy sweating or heat. Water is usually sufficient for ordinary short sessions.</p><div class="example"><b>Post-exercise replacement</b><p>The source rule recommends 2-3 cups per pound lost, so 3 lb corresponds to 6-9 cups. Modern plans individualize replacement, include sodium and avoid drinking beyond losses because overhydration can dilute blood sodium.</p></div><p>Body-mass loss is not all water in every setting, and rapid weight cutting is unsafe. Rehydrate progressively and seek care for neurological symptoms.</p><p><a href="https://www.ausport.gov.au/ais/nutrition/supplements/group_a/sports-foods2/sports-drink/how-and-when-do-i-use-it" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Australian Institute of Sport: sports drinks</a></p>']
],
questions:[
{n:1,q:"Physical activity helps to reduce a person's risk for certain chronic diseases such as high blood pressure, stroke, cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, _____, and some forms of cancer.",o:['asthma','arthritis','emphysema','osteoporosis'],a:3,e:'Osteoporosis is the source answer. Weight-bearing and resistance activity support bone and help slow age-related bone loss.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:2,q:"Increased physical activity decreases a person's risk of developing which of the following forms of cancer?",o:['Skin, lung, pancreatic, prostate, stomach, and breast (postmenopausal)','Bone, colorectal, testicular, uterine, cervical, and liver','Breast (postmenopausal), colon, lung and endometrial','Lung, skin, bone, uterine, esophageal, and liver'],a:2,e:'C is the source answer. Current evidence links activity with lower risk of additional cancers too, so the list should not be treated as exhaustive.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:3,q:"Lance's aunt is at risk for developing osteoporosis; however, if she begins weight-bearing physical activity she may experience:",o:['slower bone formation, increased progression of age-related bone loss, and reduced likelihood of injuries when falls occur.','enhanced bone formation, hastened progress of age-related bone loss, and increased likelihood of injuries when falls occur.','increased bone formation, controlled progression of age-related bone loss, and enhanced likelihood of injuries when falls occur.','stimulated bone formation, slower progression of age-related bone loss, and a reduction in the likelihood of injuries when falls occur.'],a:3,e:'Weight-bearing and resistance activity can stimulate bone, help slow age-related loss and improve strength and balance, reducing injury risk.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:4,q:'Body fat is best reduced through _____, whereas muscle mass can be increased through _____ activities.',o:['lifting weights, stretching','yoga, interval training','walking at a brisk pace, resistance training','stretching, controlled breathing'],a:2,e:'Brisk walking can increase energy expenditure, while resistance training provides the overload needed to build muscle. Fat loss still depends on sustained energy balance.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:5,q:'What type of exercise should Laura do in order to achieve cardiovascular fitness?',o:['Swimming','Weight Training','Yoga','Stretching'],a:0,e:'Swimming is sustained rhythmic aerobic activity using large muscle groups. The other activities have benefits but are not the clearest aerobic answer.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:6,q:'The _____ of a physical activity refers to the amount of physical exertion required during the activity.',o:['frequency','repetition cycle','exhaustion factor','intensity'],a:3,e:'Intensity describes how hard the body works. Frequency describes how often activity occurs.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:7,q:'Alex consumes 2700 kcalories each day. If he is "average," about how many of those calories would he expend on physical activity?',o:['405-810','825-1250','1300-1420','1425-2000'],a:0,e:'The source uses roughly 15-30%: 2,700 × 0.15-0.30 = 405-810 kcal. Actual expenditure varies widely.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:8,q:'Which of the following best describes anaerobic pathways?',o:['Complex, slow generation of ATP, able to make ATP for a long time','Simple, slow generation of ATP, able to make ATP for a short time','Complex, rapid generation of ATP, able to make ATP for a long time','Simple, rapid generation of ATP, able to make ATP for a short time'],a:3,e:'Anaerobic systems generate ATP rapidly but have limited capacity. “Simple” is textbook shorthand, not a literal description of their biochemistry.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:9,q:'Which of the following minerals is required for energy metabolism and for the maintenance, repair, and growth of muscles?',o:['Magnesium','Zinc','Iron','Calcium'],a:1,e:'The submitted course key is B, zinc, which supports protein synthesis, tissue repair and growth, and many enzyme reactions. Scientific wording caveat: magnesium also participates in energy metabolism and normal muscle function, so the choices overlap.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:10,q:'How much energy is expended by the body at rest?',o:['1 to 1.5 kcal/minute','1.5 to 2 kcal/minute','2.0 to 2.5 kcal/minute','2.5 to 3.0 kcal/minute'],a:0,e:'The source estimate is 1 to 1.5 kcal per minute. Resting expenditure varies with body size, lean mass, age and physiology.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:11,q:'Anurradha is trying to walk regularly to improve her fitness level and control her weight. Which metabolic pathways will she most likely use while walking?',o:['Citric acid cycle, β-oxidation, and the electron transport chain','Glycolysis and ATP-CP','Electron transport chain and glycolysis','Citric acid cycle, glycolysis, and ATP-CP'],a:0,e:'Steady walking relies mainly on oxidative metabolism, including the citric acid cycle, beta-oxidation and the electron transport chain.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:12,q:'Which list of pathways is correctly sequenced—left to right—as immediate, short-term, and long-term energy systems?',o:['Glycolysis, ATP-CP, citric acid cycle','Glycolysis, citric acid cycle, ATP-CP','Citric acid cycle, ATP-CP, glycolysis','ATP-CP, glycolysis, citric acid cycle'],a:3,e:'ATP-CP supplies immediate energy, glycolysis supports short intense work, and oxidative pathways dominate longer work.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:13,q:'The "fuel" for the ATP-CP pathway is _____.',o:['glucose','creatine phosphate','lactic acid','conjugated proteins'],a:1,e:'Phosphocreatine donates phosphate to ADP, rapidly regenerating ATP.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:14,q:'Brad will be competing in a 100-meter run later today. During the event, which pathways will generate most of the ATP that he needs?',o:['Glycolysis, glycogenolysis, glycogenesis','ATP-CP pathway, glycolysis','Cori cycle, protein synthesis, triglyceride synthesis','Generation of ADP, production of lactate, production of pyruvate'],a:1,e:'A short maximal sprint depends mainly on the phosphagen system and rapid glycolysis.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:15,q:'Creatine is synthesized in the _____.',o:['muscle, bone marrow, and gallbladder','liver, kidney, and pancreas','muscle, kidney, and heart','bone marrow, liver, and lungs'],a:1,e:'Creatine synthesis is an inter-organ process involving the kidney and pancreas for guanidinoacetate formation and the liver for methylation.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:16,q:'Brian is thinking of taking creatine supplements to improve his performance as a marathon runner. The dietitian advises him that creatine supplements _____.',o:['are of some benefit to athletes participating in low-intensity, endurance sports such as long-distance running','do not enhance high-intensity, short-duration athletic performance such as sprinting or power lifting; however, there is considerable evidence that they do benefit athletes participating in low-intensity, endurance sports such as long-distance running','may enhance high-intensity, short-duration athletic performance such as sprinting or power lifting; however, there is little evidence that they benefit athletes participating in low-intensity, endurance sports such as long-distance running','are of considerable benefit to athletes participating in either high-intensity, short-duration athletic events such as sprinting or power lifting or low-intensity, endurance sports such as long-distance running'],a:2,e:'Creatine is well supported for repeated short high-intensity work, strength and power, but has little direct value for steady endurance performance.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:17,q:'During prolonged activity, which of the following metabolic pathways, along with gluconeogenesis, provides glucose to active muscles?',o:['Glycolysis','Glycogenolysis','Lipolysis','Proteolysis'],a:1,e:'Liver glycogenolysis releases glucose from glycogen, while gluconeogenesis forms new glucose.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:18,q:'"Hitting the wall" is a feeling of profound fatigue that can occur during an athletic event as a result of:',o:['exhausted glycogen stores.','build-up of CO2 in the body.','a slow rate of lipolysis.','a lack of lactate for recycling'],a:0,e:'Very low glycogen availability and falling carbohydrate oxidation can cause profound fatigue and loss of pace.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:19,q:'Which type of training is best to help improve aerobic and anaerobic capacity?',o:['Strength training','Weight training','Endurance training','Interval training'],a:3,e:'The source key is D, interval training. Intervals alternate hard work and recovery, allowing both oxidative and high-rate anaerobic systems to be trained.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:20,q:'The increased production of _____ that results from training increases the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood.',o:['red blood cells','capillary blood vessels','white blood cells','endoplasmic reticula'],a:0,e:'Red blood cells carry oxygen through haemoglobin. Training also increases plasma volume and capillarization, but capillaries do not carry oxygen within the blood.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:21,q:'Which statement is true?',o:['Exercise greatly increases the micronutrient needs of athletes.','The protein needs of athletes are approximately 1.5 times those of nonathletes.','The diets recommended for athletes and nonathletes are similar.','The percentage of kcal from carbohydrate recommended by the Institute of Medicine is higher for athletes than nonathletes.'],a:2,e:'The submitted course key is C: athletes and nonathletes share the same healthy dietary foundation. Athlete quantities, timing and protein needs can differ, while the fixed “1.5 times” statement is not universally true.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:22,q:'Athletes need dietary carbohydrates for glucose and _____ stores.',o:['triglyceride','glycerol','protein','glycogen'],a:3,e:'Carbohydrate replenishes liver and muscle glycogen, a key fuel for moderate- to high-intensity work.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:23,q:"According to the Institute of Medicine's Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range, what percentage of an athlete's total calories should come from carbohydrates?",o:['35-55','20-35','60-85','45-65'],a:3,e:'The Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range for carbohydrate is 45-65% of total energy. Sport-specific plans also use grams per kilogram.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:24,q:'_____ is key for oxygen delivery to tissues and _____ is needed for building and repairing bone tissue.',o:['Iron, calcium','Iron, zinc','Selenium, vitamin D','Calcium, magnesium'],a:0,e:'Iron is required for haemoglobin and oxygen transport; calcium is a principal mineral in bone tissue.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:25,q:'How many liters of water do sedentary adult women and men need per day?',o:['2.0 (women), 2.7 (men)','2.3 (women), 3.2 (men)','2.7 (women), 3.7 (men)','3.0 (women), 4.0 (men)'],a:2,e:'The reference values are 2.7 L for women and 3.7 L for men. They include water from food and all beverages.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:26,q:'What would probably happen to an athlete if he became dehydrated and lost 5% of his body weight by sweating?',o:['An increase in blood volume','A decrease in core body temperature','A rapid and weak pulse','High blood pressure'],a:2,e:'Severe dehydration lowers circulating volume and increases cardiovascular strain, producing a rapid, weak pulse.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:27,q:'What condition is characterized by a loss of 7% to 10% of body weight, dry skin, confusion, and loss of consciousness?',o:['Heat stroke','Dehydration','Heat exhaustion','Sun poisoning'],a:0,e:'The submitted course key is A, heat stroke. Confusion and loss of consciousness are neurological red flags requiring emergency cooling and medical help. Severe dehydration can coexist, so the symptoms overlap.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:28,q:'When would it be beneficial for an athlete to consume a sports drink containing carbohydrates and electrolytes?',o:['It is never beneficial','During intense training sessions lasting less than one hour','During workouts/events that last longer than one hour','It is always beneficial'],a:2,e:'Longer exercise is the source answer. Shorter high-intensity work, heat and heavy sweating can also justify a targeted plan.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:29,q:'Jeff has completed his track event, and in the process lost 3 pounds of his body weight. How many cups of fluid does he need to drink?',o:['2-4','6-9','10-12','1-2'],a:1,e:'At 2-3 cups per pound, 3 lb corresponds to 6-9 cups. Modern rehydration plans are individualized and should avoid overdrinking.',unscored:false,image:null},
{n:30,q:'Muscles rely solely on their stored glycogen for ATP during physical activity.',o:['True','False'],a:1,e:'False. Muscles can use blood glucose, glycogen, fatty acids and smaller contributions from other fuels, depending on intensity and duration.',unscored:false,image:null}
],
cards:[['Aerobic exercise','Rhythmic sustained activity that develops cardiorespiratory fitness.'],['Resistance training','Progressive overload used to build strength and muscle.'],['Weight-bearing activity','Loads the skeleton and helps support bone health.'],['Intensity','The amount of exertion required during an activity.'],['FITT','Frequency, intensity, time and type.'],['Physical activity expenditure','Highly variable; the source uses about 15-30% for an average adult.'],['ATP-CP system','Immediate, very rapid energy with very limited capacity.'],['Phosphocreatine','Donates phosphate to ADP to regenerate ATP quickly.'],['Anaerobic glycolysis','Rapid ATP from glucose or glycogen for short intense work.'],['Oxidative metabolism','High-capacity ATP production for longer activity.'],['Beta-oxidation','Breakdown of fatty acids to acetyl-CoA.'],['Electron transport chain','Uses reducing equivalents and oxygen to support ATP synthesis.'],['Creatine synthesis','An inter-organ pathway involving kidney, pancreas and liver.'],['Creatine performance','Most useful for repeated high-intensity work, strength and power.'],['Glycogenolysis','Breakdown of glycogen; the liver helps maintain blood glucose.'],['Hitting the wall','Profound endurance fatigue associated with very low glycogen availability.'],['Interval training','Alternates hard work and recovery to train multiple energy systems.'],['Red blood cells','Carry oxygen through haemoglobin.'],['Athlete diet foundation','Adequate energy, varied foods, carbohydrate, quality protein, fats and micronutrients.'],['Carbohydrate AMDR','45-65% of total energy for adults.'],['Glycogen','Stored carbohydrate in liver and muscle.'],['Total water AI','About 2.7 L/day for women and 3.7 L/day for men, including food.'],['Sports drink','Fluid, carbohydrate and electrolytes for targeted training or competition needs.'],['Severe heat symptoms','Confusion or unconsciousness requires emergency response.']]
};
